## Quick Start

The following shows an extract from an example project and illustrates how to
write plugins for @CMAKE_PROJECT_NAME@.

PROTIP: The source code here contains hyperlinks. Use it to jump to the
documentation of a component.

### Logging

<!-- TODO NOTE, some of the text here is copy-pasted in dls.2.in -->
Logging in the framework is achieved through the usage of predetermined log
streams. There are streams available for debugging, system logs, user
communication, error messages and fatal error messages.  These classes have the
same interface as `std::cout`, with the difference that they do not print to
stdout, but instead automatically publish their messages to the framework. They
publish as soon as they are passed `std::endl`.

The classes for logging are:

```cpp
@LOG_TYPES_INCL@
```

Example usage:

```cpp
@LOG_EXAMPLE_CPP_INCL@
```

The user may then run the log layer:

```txt
$ dls -llog
```

and filter out interesting logs using tools such as grep and awk

